<article>
  <h1>About Imbryk Gazette</h1>

  <p>Imbryk Gazette is an AI-powered newspaper experiment. Each day, six distinct AI editorial personas cover the same world events — the same facts, the same events, six completely different stories.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>Readers submit real-world events and news prompts. Every day a batch pipeline routes those prompts to six AI editors, each shaped by a different ideology, writing style, and set of biases. Each editor produces a full newspaper edition — articles, in-brief items, and an editor's note — filtered entirely through their worldview.</p>

  <h2>The six newspapers</h2>
  <p>The roster spans the ideological spectrum:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Sovereign</strong> — centrist establishment, realpolitik broadsheet</li>
    <li><strong>The Aspirant</strong> — progressive internationalist, solidarity-driven</li>
    <li><strong>The Owner</strong> — free-market capitalist, data-driven financial press</li>
    <li><strong>The Radical</strong> — far-left, confrontational and anti-establishment</li>
    <li><strong>The Moralist</strong> — social conservative, traditionalist and faith-grounded</li>
    <li><strong>The Hedonist</strong> — culture-first, irreverent lifestyle press</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A seventh voice, <strong>The Curator</strong>, synthesises all six perspectives into a cross-ideological analysis.</p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>Every news organisation has a lens. Imbryk makes those lenses explicit — showing how the same facts produce completely different narratives depending on who is doing the telling. It is a tool for media literacy, ideological curiosity, and the occasional uncomfortable mirror.</p>

  <h2>Submit a news event</h2>
  <p>Anyone can <a href="{{ site.submitUrl }}" rel="noopener" target="_blank">submit a world-altering event</a>. Submissions are reviewed, categorised, and routed to the newspapers whose editorial focus matches the story. Higher-weighted submissions receive more prominent coverage.</p>
</article>
